Yes, you can build it on WhatsApp. Chat SDK has an **official WhatsApp adapter** (`@chat-adapter/whatsapp`) that uses Meta's WhatsApp Business Cloud API. It plugs into Mastra the same way as the other platforms.

## Architecture

```
User's WhatsApp ──► Meta Cloud API ──webhook──► your server (/api/webhooks/whatsapp)
                                                   │  Chat SDK + @chat-adapter/whatsapp
                                                   ▼
                                    identify({sessionId: thread.id}) ──► Mastra agent (wrapMastra)
                                                   │                        tools, memory, steps
                                                   ▼                              │
                                        thread.post(reply) ◄──────────────────────┘
                                                              all traced in neatlogs
```

## What you need from Meta (free, about 20 minutes)
1. Go to **developers.facebook.com/apps**, create a **Business** app and add the **WhatsApp** product.
2. Meta gives you a **free test phone number**, so you don't need your own business number. In development mode you can message up to 5 verified numbers; add your own phone as one of them.
3. Copy these four values into your `.env`:

| Variable | Where to find it |
|---|---|
| `WHATSAPP_ACCESS_TOKEN` | WhatsApp → API Setup. ⚠️ This token **expires after 24 hours**. For the full 48-hour hackathon, create a permanent **System User Token** |
| `WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID` | WhatsApp → API Setup |
| `WHATSAPP_APP_SECRET` | App Settings → Basic |
| `WHATSAPP_VERIFY_TOKEN` | Any random string you choose |

4. **The webhook needs a public HTTPS URL.** Locally, use `cloudflared tunnel` or `ngrok`, or deploy to Vercel. Then, in **WhatsApp → Configuration**:
   - Set the URL to `https://…/api/webhooks/whatsapp`.
   - Set the verify token to the same random string.
   - Subscribe to `messages` and `user_id_update`.

## The code in outline

```ts
// lib/bot.ts
import { Chat } from 'chat';
import { createWhatsAppAdapter } from '@chat-adapter/whatsapp';
import { createMemoryState } from '@chat-adapter/state-memory';
import { identify } from 'neatlogs';
import { agent } from './agent';          // wrapMastra(new Agent({...}))

export const bot = new Chat({
  userName: 'mybot',
  adapters: { whatsapp: createWhatsAppAdapter() },
  state: createMemoryState(),             // use Redis/Postgres for anything beyond local dev
});

bot.onDirectMessage(async (thread, message) => {
  await thread.markAsRead();
  await thread.startTyping();
  const reply = await identify(
    { sessionId: thread.id, endUserId: thread.id },    // one neatlogs session per WhatsApp chat
    async () => (await agent.generate(message.text)).text,
  );
  await thread.post({ markdown: reply });
});
```

## WhatsApp limits that affect the design
- **Streaming is buffered.** The adapter sends the finished reply, not a token-by-token stream, so use `agent.generate()`.
- **The 24-hour rule.** You can only reply freely within 24 hours of the user's last message. To message first, you need Meta-approved template messages.
- **Long tasks.** Meta's webhook expects a fast reply, so return immediately (`waitUntil`), post "working on it…", and send the result when it's done. This is also a natural way to show "complete a substantial task end to end" in your demo.
- **Formatting.** Messages over 4,096 characters are split automatically. Cards support at most 3 reply buttons, and files (PDF, images, audio) can be sent.

## Packages
```bash
npm i @mastra/core neatlogs chat @chat-adapter/whatsapp @chat-adapter/state-memory zod
npm i @openrouter/ai-sdk-provider   # or @ai-sdk/deepseek, depending on your key
```

Next step: create the Meta app and get the four values. Meanwhile I can scaffold the project (Mastra agent, WhatsApp webhook, neatlogs and a tunnel script) so it's ready when your keys are. Tell me **what task the bot should complete** and **which LLM key you're using**.
